# Block model UV mapping

When writing or editing Minecraft Java block/item element models for Wrights:

- Prefer mapping each visible face with the **full texture**, starting at `[0, 0]`.
- For a normal 16×16 panel face, use `"uv": [0, 0, 16, 16]`.
- Do **not** crop UVs to the cube’s world position (for example `[1, 4, 15, 14]` or `[2, 5, 14, 13]`) just because the element is inset. That only shows a slice of the art and looks wrong on factory panel textures.
- Thin edge faces may stretch a strip from the top-left of the texture (for example `[0, 0, 16, 2]`), still starting at `0, 0`.
- Keep separate texture keys (`#front`, `#side`, `#top`, …) when the art differs per face; full UV does not mean one shared atlas crop.

```json
// BAD — cropped mid-texture
"north": {"uv": [1, 4, 15, 14], "texture": "#front"}

// GOOD — full texture on the face
"north": {"uv": [0, 0, 16, 16], "texture": "#front"}
```
